1.13.1 Columns
--------------

Ten columns are available; five are displayed by default:

   Ten columns are available; five are displayed by default.  They are
listed here in registry order, which is also the order a column returns
to when it is shown again:

Column        Shows                                       Width   Default
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘todo’        TODO keyword                                6       yes
‘date’        modification time of the node's file        10      yes
‘tags’        tags, comma-separated                       20      yes
‘priority’    letter of the priority cookie               1       no
‘scheduled’   scheduled date                              10      no
‘deadline’    deadline date                               10      no
‘level’       outline level; 0 for a file-level node      1       no
‘olp’         outline path above the node's own heading   30      no
‘title’       node title                                  50      yes
‘file’        file, relative to ‘org-roam-directory’      30      yes

   ‘title’ and ‘file’ are last in the registry on purpose: they are the
two free-form columns, so turning any other column on places it *before*
them rather than pushing them out of their usual position.

   ‘org-roam-gt-list-columns’ sets which columns are displayed and their
left-to-right order:

     (setq org-roam-gt-list-columns '(todo date tags title file))

   Within a buffer, ‘c’ shows or hides one column and ‘C’ sets the whole
list and its order.

   Each column is bounded by its width.  A value that does not fit is
truncated to the column and marked with ‘truncate-string-ellipsis’
(‘…’), so a shortened cell is visibly shortened rather than silently
cut.  Raise a width to see more:

     (setq org-roam-gt-list-column-widths '((title . 80) (file . 60)))

   The *last* displayed column is the exception: it has the rest of the
line to itself, with nothing after it to push out of place, so it is
shown in full and its width is ignored.  Put the column whose values run
longest at the end — with the default columns that is ‘file’, so full
paths stay readable.

   The widths are chosen so the fixed part of a row fits in about 90
columns, leaving the rest of the frame to the last column.  Tags are
comma-separated rather than space-separated: once a cell is truncated,
space-separated tags read as one run of text.

   The ‘date’ column is the modification time of the file the node lives
in, so every node in one file shows the same date.  Its format is
‘org-roam-gt-list-date-format’ (default ‘"%Y-%m-%d"’); a format whose
fields run from the largest unit to the smallest keeps the column's sort
chronological.


