1.13.2 Sorting and filtering
----------------------------

Click a column header to sort by it, or press ‘s’ to cycle through the
sortable columns.  The initial sort is
‘org-roam-gt-list-default-sort-key’, which defaults to ‘("Date" . t)’ —
most recently modified first.  Hiding the column a buffer is sorted by
falls back to the first sortable column rather than signalling.

   ‘/’ adds a filter.  Selecting on more than one attribute is a matter
of applying more than one filter — there are two ways to combine them,
and they do opposite things:

   • *Several values in one filter widen the selection.*  The tag, TODO,
     and level readers take a comma-separated list, and a node matches
     when *any* of the values does.  Reading ‘jp,ww’ at the tag prompt
     selects everything tagged jp or ww.

   • *Several filters narrow it.*  Each filter applies on top of the
     ones already in force, so a tag filter followed by a level filter
     selects the nodes that satisfy both.  The same filter may be
     applied repeatedly: two separate ‘by-tag’ filters select the nodes
     carrying *both* tags, where one ‘by-tag’ with two values selects
     the nodes carrying *either*.

   With a prefix argument (‘C-u /’) the filter is negated and selects
exactly what it would otherwise reject, e.g.  ‘C-u /’ ‘by-todo’ ‘DONE’
hides done nodes.  A negated multi-value filter is inverted as a whole:
negating tags ‘jp,ww’ selects the nodes carrying neither.

   ‘D’ removes a single filter, with completion over the ones in force;
‘V’ clears them all along with the rest of the view.

   The mode line lists the active filters and how many rows they leave:

     -:--- *Org Roam Nodes*   [tag=jp,ww !todo=DONE] 25/2850

Filter              Selects                                    Values
------------------------------------------------------------------------
‘by-tag’            nodes carrying one of the tags             many
‘by-todo’           nodes with one of the TODO keywords        many
‘by-level’          file-level nodes, heading nodes, or both   many
‘by-title-regexp’   nodes whose title matches a regexp         one
‘by-file-regexp’    nodes whose file name matches a regexp     one
‘unfilter’          clears every active filter                 —

   The regexp filters take one value rather than a list, because a comma
is an ordinary character inside a regexp and splitting on it would
corrupt the pattern; apply the filter twice to narrow further.

   The tag and TODO readers offer only what the nodes on display
actually carry, so a completion candidate never produces an empty
buffer.

   Filters are a registry, ‘org-roam-gt-list-filter-alist’; each entry
is ‘(KEY :name STRING :reader FN :predicate FN :doc STRING)’.  The
reader takes no arguments and returns a *list* of values, even when it
read only one.  The predicate is called as ‘(NODE VALUE)’ with a single
value and returns non-nil when the node matches it — combining the
values and applying negation is done once by the list buffer, not in
every predicate, so a predicate you add gets both behaviours for free.
Adding your own filter is a matter of pushing an entry onto that alist.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: Keys,  Next: Bookmarking a view,  Prev: Sorting and filtering,  Up: Node list buffer

1.13.3 Keys
-----------

Key         Action
------------------------------------------------------
‘RET’       visit the node (same window)
‘o’         visit the node (other window)
‘TAB’       preview, keeping point in the list
‘i’         pretty-print the node record
‘s’         cycle the sort column
‘/’         add a filter
‘C-u /’     add a negated filter
‘D’         remove one filter
‘c’         show or hide one column
‘C’         set the whole column list and its order
‘V’         reset sort, filters, and columns
‘g’         re-read the database, then redraw
‘C-x r m’   bookmark the current view
‘q’         quit

   The database is read once per ‘g’ and cached for the buffer, so
sorting, filtering, and column changes redraw without querying it again.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: Bookmarking a view,  Next: Remembering the view,  Prev: Keys,  Up: Node list buffer

1.13.4 Bookmarking a view
-------------------------

A bookmark on this buffer records the *view*, not a position: the
displayed columns, the active filters, and the sort key.  A position
would be meaningless, since the row under point moves whenever the
database changes.

   Press ‘C-x r m’ (‘bookmark-set’) in the list buffer.  The default
name offered is ‘org-roam-gt-list-bookmark-prefix’ with the active
filters appended, so several saved views stay distinguishable:

     Org roam nodes: by-tag=dmgTodo by-level=heading

   Jumping to the bookmark opens the list buffer if needed, applies the
saved columns, filters, and sort key, and re-reads the database — so the
restored view shows the nodes as they are then, not as they were when
the bookmark was stored.

   This is built on ‘bookmark.el’ (a ‘bookmark-make-record-function’ and
a ‘handler’ property), so any bookmark front end built on ‘bookmark.el’
stores and jumps to these without needing to know anything about
org-roam-gt.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: Remembering the view,  Prev: Bookmarking a view,  Up: Node list buffer

1.13.5 Remembering the view
---------------------------

The sort key, the displayed columns, and the active filters are kept for
as long as the buffer lives.  To carry them across Emacs sessions:

     (setq org-roam-gt-list-persist-state t)

   They are then written to ‘org-roam-gt-list-state-file’ whenever they
change and re-read the next time the mode is entered.  Point is not
persisted.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: Development,  Next: MELPA submission,  Prev: Node list buffer,  Up: org-roam-gt

1.14 Development
================

The repository ships a ‘Makefile’ that drives every dev workflow via a
project-local ‘.elpa/’ (nothing touches your user package directory):

     make              # byte-compile
     make test         # run the buttercup test suite
     make lint         # package-lint
     make checkdoc     # checkdoc (errors on any warning)
     make check-declare  # verify declare-function arguments
     make check        # compile + lint + checkdoc + check-declare
     make clean        # remove *.elc

   The GitHub Actions workflow at ‘.github/workflows/package-lint.yml’
runs ‘make lint’, ‘make checkdoc’, ‘make check-declare’, and ‘make test’
on Emacs 30.1, 31.1, and snapshot.


File: org-roam-gt.info,  Node: MELPA submission,  Prev: Development,  Up: org-roam-gt

1.15 MELPA submission
=====================

The MELPA recipe lives at ‘melpa/org-roam-gt’; instructions for
submitting it upstream are in ‘melpa/README.md’.


